<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Construction of DEX Contour Plot
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
The four vertex values of this imbedded 2<sup>2</sup> full
factorial design involving X1 and X2 were obtained from the
raw data by simple averaging.  Thus the value on the contour
plot at X1 = -1 and X2 = -1 was gotten by referencing the
raw data table and noting all (X1 = -1, X2 = -1) data;
there were 2 such data values: Y = 1.51  and Y = 1.70.
Averaging these two values together yields the 1.6 which in
turn appears on the plot.  We proceed similarly for the other
three vertices.  To draw the contour lines, we make use of the
local 2-term equation in X1 and X2 only, namely:
<P>
<UL>
   Yhat = mu + 0.5(B1*X1 + B2*X2 + B12*X1*X2)
   Yhat = 2.65875 + 0.5
</UL>
<P>
where the coefficients B. were drawn from the Yates analysis.
From the raw data table, we note that the 8 data values ranged
from ...  to ..., and so contour lines were drawn
from ...  to ..., at increments of ...  .

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Information From the DEX Countour Plot
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
From the contour plot, we may derive the following
3 pieces of information:
<P>
<OL>
   <li>Interaction significance;
   <li>Best (data) setting for these 2 dominant factors;
   <li>Best (non-data) settings.
</OL>

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Interaction Significance
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
Note the appearance of the contour plot.  If the contour
curves are linear, then that implies that the interaction
term is insignificant; if the contour curves have considerable
curvature, than that implies that the interaction term is
large and important.  In our case, the contour curves are
linear and so we conclude that the X1*X2 term is
insignificant.  That is, we conclude that the X1 effect
(= ...  units) is valid irrespective of the settings of X2.

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Best Settings
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
To determine the best (data) setting (that is, the best factor
settings for the already-run experiment), then we first must
define what "best" means.  As before, for this case study,
"best" here means to <b>maximize</b> (rather than minimize or
hit a target) the response.  Hence from the contour plot, we
determine the best settings for the two dominant factors by
simply scanning the four vertices and choosing the vertex
with the <b>largest</b> value (= average response).  In this
case, it is (X1 = +1, X2 = +1).  Hence the best settings for
the two dominant factors based on the experiment that was
already run is
<P>
<UL>
   (X1 = +1, X2 = +1).
</UL>
<P>
As for factor X3, the contour plot provides no best setting
info, and so we would resort to other tools: the main effects
plot, the interaction effects matrix, or the ordered data
(see next section) to determine optimal X3 settings.

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Best Setting (non-data)
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
To determine the best (non-data) setting (that is, the best
setting even beyond the settings utilized in the already-run
experiment), we do the following 6 steps:
<P>
<OL>
   <li>Note Best Current Value;
   <li>Specify Target Future Value;
   <li>Note Target Contour Curve;
   <li>Determine Closest Point on Target Contour Curve;
   <li>Translate Optimal Settings to Original Factor Units;
   <li>Confirmation Run or Confirmation Experiment.
</OL>
<P>

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Note Best Current Value
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
We first go to the best data setting.  That is, we use as
our starting point that vertex (out of the four) with the
highest average response.  For the case study at hand, that
optimal point is: (X1 = +1, X2 = +1).

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Specify Target Future Value
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
We mentally specify as to what value of the response (above
and beyond our best data vertex) that we would like to see
if we had the luxury of sampling elsewhere in the factor
domains.  For our case study, we can already achieve (on the
average) ..., and so let us suppose we choose to increase our
response to ... -- we thus specify ... as the "target future
value".

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Note Target Contour Curve
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
We note the plot contour curve which comes closest to the
target future value.  Our next best setting is thus going to be
somewhere along the Y = ...  contour curve.  In our case, with
the target future value at ..., and with the contour curves
at ..., ..., ..., to ...  we thus choose contour curve
Y = ... as the target curve.

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Determine Closest Point On Target Contour Curve
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
Since the contour curves were based on the data and since the
validity of the contour curves becomes more and more suspect as
we move away from the data vertices, then the strategy is to
choose that value on the target contour curve which is closest
to the optimal data vertex.  In practice this is achieved by
starting at this best vertex and proceeding in the direction of
the path of steepest ascent (noting the nature and curvature of
the local contour curves) until we (perpendicularly) intersect
the target contour curve.  The interesection point becomes our
best "estimate" for the X1 and X2 settings that will yield the
desired future value.  This is the point where we would like to
conduct our next experiment, or at least run confirmation
runs to determine if this best-estimate point in fact yields
the quality of response that we desire.

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Translate Optimal Settings to Original Factor Units
</I>
</TD>
<TD WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
At this intersection point, note the coded settings for factors
X1 and X2.  Since the original four vertices were coded
values of +-1 in each of the two factors under study, it is
quite common for the best new settings to be somewhere in the
region +- 1.25 to +- 2. Determine exactly such best settings
in coded units, and then linearly translate back to the best
settings in the original factor units.  For example, in our
case, if the optimal new setting for X1 (= number of wire
turns) was X1 = +2, and since
<P>
<UL>
   X1 = -1 corresponds to  90 turns, and<br>
   X1 = +1 corresponds to 180 turns, then<br>
   X1 = +2 corresponds to 225 turns.<br>
</UL>
<P>
Similarly, if X2 (winding distance) = +2
is optimal, then since
<P>
<UL>
   X2 = -1 corresponds to  .38, and<br>
   X2 = +1 corresponds to 1.14, this implies<br>
   X2 = +2 corresponds to 1.52.<br>
</UL>

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->

<!-- begin paragraph -->
<TR>
<TD WIDTH=15% VALIGN=top>
<!-- Add marginal notes below -->
<I>
Do Confirmation Run or Confirmation Experiment
</I>
</TD>
<TD VALIGN=TOP WIDTH=85%>
<!-- Add main text below -->
 Test the above best setting by additionally collecting data
at this new point, or running a 2-level factorial experiment
(centered at this point) in the vicinity of this new point (and
repeat the entire 2-level analysis again).

</TD>
</TR>
<!-- end paragraph -->
